\section*{Question 3}

A test plane flies in a straight line with positive velocity \(v(t)\), in miles per minute at time \(t\) minutes, where \(v\) is a differentiable function of \(t\). Selected values of \(v(t)\) for \(0 \leq t \leq 40\) are shown in the table above.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Midpoint Riemann sum is
\[
\begin{aligned}
& 10 \cdot[v(5)+v(15)+v(25)+v(35)] \\
& =10 \cdot[9.2+7.0+2.4+4.3]=229
\end{aligned}
\]
The integral gives the total distance in miles that the plane flies during the 40 minutes.
\item[(b)] By the Mean Value Theorem, \(v^{\prime}(t)=0\) somewhere in the interval \((0,15)\) and somewhere in the interval (25, 30). Therefore the acceleration will equal 0 for at least two values of \(t\).
\item[(c)] \(f^{\prime}(23)=-0.407\) or -0.408 miles per minute \({ }^{2}\)
\item[(d)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text { Average velocity } & =\frac{1}{40} \int_{0}^{40} f(t) d t \\
& =5.916 \text { miles per minute }
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}
